window.MORE=(window.MORE||[]).concat([
{id:"calc",t:"Calculus",c:"Mathematics",i:"∫",p:[["Origins","Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz developed calculus independently in the late 1600s, and a bitter dispute over credit followed."],["Two ideas","Differential calculus studies rates of change, such as speed, while integral calculus adds up quantities, such as area. The fundamental theorem of calculus links the two."],["Uses","Physics, engineering, economics and medicine all use calculus to model change."]],r:["newton","euclid"]},
{id:"alg",t:"Algebra",c:"Mathematics",i:"✖️",p:[["Name","The word algebra comes from al-jabr, a term in a book written around 820 CE by the Persian scholar al-Khwarizmi."],["Method","Algebra uses letters for unknown numbers, so that rules for solving equations apply to whole families of problems."],["Notation","Symbols such as the equals sign and x for unknowns became standard in the 1500s and 1600s through writers including Robert Recorde and René Descartes."]],r:["zero","silk"]},
{id:"stats",t:"Statistics",c:"Mathematics",i:"📊",p:[["Averages","The mean, median and mode summarize a set of numbers in different ways, and each can mislead if the data are skewed."],["Pioneers","Florence Nightingale used charts to show that sanitation could save soldiers’ lives, and the bell-shaped normal distribution describes many natural measurements."],["Sampling","Surveys study a sample of a population and report a margin of error, which tells how much the result might differ from the truth."]],r:["prob","ai"]},
{id:"fib",t:"Fibonacci and the Golden Ratio",c:"Mathematics",i:"🐚",p:[["The sequence","Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two before it: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13. Leonardo of Pisa, called Fibonacci, popularized it in Liber Abaci in 1202."],["Golden ratio","The ratio of successive Fibonacci numbers approaches about 1.618, known as the golden ratio."],["In nature","Fibonacci numbers do appear in the spirals of some sunflowers and pinecones, though many popular claims about the golden ratio in art and nature are exaggerated."]],r:["zero","eco"]},
{id:"pi",t:"Pi",c:"Mathematics",i:"π",p:[["The constant","Pi is the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its diameter, about 3.14159, and the same for every circle."],["History","Archimedes bounded pi between 223/71 and 22/7 around 250 BCE. The symbol π was popularized by Leonhard Euler in the 1700s."],["Irrational","Johann Lambert proved in 1761 that pi is irrational, so its digits never end or repeat. Pi Day is celebrated on 14 March."]],r:["euclid","greece"]},
{id:"infin",t:"Infinity",c:"Mathematics",i:"♾️",p:[["The symbol","John Wallis introduced the symbol ∞ in 1655, and mathematicians long argued about whether infinity could be treated as a number."],["Cantor","In the 1870s and 1880s Georg Cantor showed that some infinities are bigger than others. The whole numbers and the real numbers have different sizes of infinity."],["Hilbert’s hotel","David Hilbert’s thought experiment of a fully booked hotel with infinitely many rooms shows how infinite sets can behave in surprising ways."]],r:["euclid","prime"]},
{id:"hindu",t:"Hinduism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🪔",p:[["Roots","Hinduism developed in the Indian subcontinent over thousands of years and has no single founder. Its oldest texts are the Vedas."],["Ideas","Central ideas include dharma, or duty, karma, the effect of actions, and moksha, liberation from the cycle of rebirth. Worship takes many forms, with deities such as Vishnu, Shiva and Devi."],["Today","Hinduism has more than a billion followers, mostly in India and Nepal, and its festivals include Diwali, the festival of lights, and Holi."]],r:["relig","buddh"]},
{id:"islam",t:"Islam",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🕋",p:[["Origins","Islam began in 7th-century Arabia with the revelations received by the prophet Muhammad, recorded in the Quran. The Islamic calendar starts with the Hijra, his move to Medina in 622 CE."],["Five Pillars","Muslims observe five pillars: the declaration of faith, prayer, charity, fasting in Ramadan and, if able, pilgrimage to Mecca."],["Communities","Islam has roughly two billion followers, with Sunni and Shia as its two main branches, and Indonesia has the largest Muslim population."]],r:["relig","sulu"]},
{id:"christ",t:"Christianity",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"✝️",p:[["Beginnings","Christianity grew from the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth in the 1st century, and its scriptures are the Bible, with the Old and New Testaments."],["History","It spread through the Roman Empire, and the Edict of Milan in 313 ended persecution. The Great Schism of 1054 split the Eastern and Western churches, and the Reformation began in 1517."],["Today","It is the world’s largest religion, with roughly 2.4 billion followers, and includes Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant traditions."]],r:["relig","reform"]},
{id:"judaism",t:"Judaism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"✡️",p:[["Origins","Judaism is one of the oldest monotheistic religions, tracing its roots to the ancient Israelites. Its central text is the Torah."],["Practice","The Talmud records centuries of rabbinic debate, and the weekly Sabbath and festivals such as Passover mark the Jewish year."],["Diaspora","After the Romans destroyed the Second Temple in 70 CE, Jewish communities spread across the world, and today about 15 million people identify as Jewish."]],r:["relig","rome"]},
{id:"stoic",t:"Stoicism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🏺",p:[["Origins","Zeno of Citium founded Stoicism in Athens around 300 BCE, teaching in a painted colonnade called the Stoa."],["Ideas","Stoics held that virtue is the highest good and that we should focus on what we can control while accepting what we cannot."],["Writers","The slave Epictetus, the statesman Seneca and the emperor Marcus Aurelius were its best-known Roman voices, and Marcus’s Meditations remain widely read."]],r:["socr","rome"]},
{id:"tao",t:"Taoism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"☯️",p:[["The Way","Taoism centers on the Tao, or the way, an underlying principle of nature. The Tao Te Ching, traditionally credited to Laozi, is its foundational text."],["Ideas","Wu wei, or effortless action, urges living in harmony with nature, and the yin-yang symbol expresses balance between complementary forces."],["Zhuangzi","The thinker Zhuangzi expanded these ideas in stories full of humor and paradox, and Taoism later grew into an organized religion with temples and rituals."]],r:["confu","china"]},
{id:"elephant",t:"Elephants",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐘",p:[["Giants","The African bush elephant is the largest land animal, with big males weighing up to about 6 metric tons. Asian elephants are somewhat smaller."],["Family life","Herds are led by an older female, the matriarch, and a calf is born after a pregnancy of about 22 months, the longest of any land animal."],["Threats","Poaching for ivory and loss of habitat have cut elephant numbers, and conservation programs now protect many populations."]],r:["eco","phbio"]},
{id:"whale",t:"Whales and Dolphins",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐋",p:[["Giants of the sea","The blue whale, which can reach about 30 m, is the largest animal known to have ever lived."],["Two groups","Baleen whales filter tiny animals from the water, while toothed whales such as dolphins and orcas hunt fish and squid, often using echolocation."],["Protection","The International Whaling Commission’s moratorium on commercial whaling took effect in 1986, and several species have since been recovering."]],r:["pacific","reef"]},
{id:"insect",t:"Insects",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐝",p:[["Diversity","Insects have six legs and three body parts, and more than a million species have been described, roughly half of all known living things."],["Life cycles","Many insects change form completely, from egg to larva to pupa to adult, as a butterfly does."],["Importance","Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, while ants, beetles and termites recycle dead material."]],r:["eco","evo"]},
{id:"shark",t:"Sharks",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦈",p:[["Ancient fish","Sharks have existed for more than 400 million years, and about 500 species are known."],["Body","Their skeletons are made of cartilage, not bone, and their skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales."],["Sizes","The whale shark, the largest fish, can pass 12 m but feeds on plankton, while great whites are fearsome hunters of seals."]],r:["reef","pacific"]},
{id:"mars",t:"Mars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔴",p:[["The red planet","Mars looks red because its surface dust is rich in iron oxide. Its thin atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide."],["Landforms","Olympus Mons, about 22 km high, is the tallest volcano in the Solar System, and the Valles Marineris canyon system stretches thousands of kilometers."],["Exploration","NASA’s Perseverance rover landed in February 2021, and its helicopter Ingenuity made the first powered flight on another planet in April 2021."]],r:["solar","space"]},
{id:"exoplanet",t:"Exoplanets",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",p:[["Discovery","In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz found the first planet orbiting a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, and won a share of the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Methods","Astronomers find exoplanets by the slight dimming of a star as a planet passes in front of it and by the wobble a planet causes in its star."],["Counts","More than 5,000 exoplanets have been confirmed, many by the Kepler mission launched in 2009, and some lie in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist."]],r:["stars","scope"]},
{id:"comet",t:"Comets and Asteroids",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☄️",p:[["Comets","Comets are icy bodies that grow glowing tails when they near the Sun, and the tails always point away from it. Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years and is next due in 2061."],["Asteroids","Most asteroids lie in a belt between Mars and Jupiter. An asteroid impact about 66 million years ago helped end the age of the dinosaurs."],["Missions","In 2014 the Rosetta mission put the lander Philae on comet 67P, and in 2022 NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately struck the asteroid moonlet Dimorphos to change its orbit."]],r:["solar","space"]},
{id:"bigbang",t:"The Big Bang",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",p:[["The theory","The universe began expanding about 13.8 billion years ago from an extremely hot, dense state."],["Evidence","Edwin Hubble found in 1929 that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and in 1965 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson detected the cosmic microwave background, the leftover glow of the early universe."],["First elements","In the first minutes the universe made mostly hydrogen and helium, and later generations of stars forged heavier elements."]],r:["stars","relat"]}]);
