/* data/extra-sections12.js */
// Adds one more section to each article below (appended, so earlier sections are kept).
window.EXTRA=window.EXTRA||{};
(function(x){for(const k in x)window.EXTRA[k]=(window.EXTRA[k]||[]).concat(x[k])})({
solar:[["Exploration","Every planet has been visited by at least one spacecraft. Voyager 2 is the only probe to fly past Uranus (1986) and Neptune (1989), and New Horizons flew past Pluto in 2015."]],
photo:[["Light and dark reactions","Photosynthesis happens in two stages. The light-dependent reactions capture energy and split water, releasing oxygen, and the Calvin cycle then uses that energy to build sugar from carbon dioxide."]],
dna:[["Copying and mutation","Before a cell divides, its DNA unzips and each strand serves as a template for a new partner, so both new cells get a full copy. Rare copying mistakes, called mutations, are the raw material of evolution."]],
bh:[["Hawking radiation","In 1974 Stephen Hawking predicted that black holes slowly give off a faint glow, now called Hawking radiation. For black holes of stellar size it is far too weak to detect."]],
egypt:[["Religion and the afterlife","Egyptians worshipped many gods, such as Ra, the sun god, and Osiris, ruler of the dead. They mummified the dead to preserve the body for the afterlife, and the tomb of Tutankhamun, found almost intact in 1922, revealed spectacular burial treasures."]],
rome:[["Engineering","Romans built more than 80,000 km of paved roads and aqueducts that carried water to cities. Their concrete let them raise structures such as the Pantheon, whose unreinforced dome is still the largest of its kind."]],
indrev:[["Spread","Industrialization spread from Britain to Belgium, France, Germany and the United States in the 1800s, and to Japan after 1868. A second wave from about 1870 brought steel, chemicals and electricity."]],
amazon:[["The river","The Amazon River carries more water than any other river, about a fifth of all river water that flows into the oceans. It has no bridges along its main channel."]],
tect:[["Hotspots","Some volcanoes form far from plate edges, above hot plumes rising from deep in the mantle. As the Pacific Plate moves over one such hotspot, it has built the chain of the Hawaiian Islands."]],
internet:[["Mobile and social","Smartphones made the internet something most people carry with them. By the 2020s about two thirds of the world’s population was online, and many people spend several hours a day on social media."]],
evo:[["Human evolution","Modern humans, Homo sapiens, appeared in Africa about 300,000 years ago and later spread worldwide. Other human species such as Neanderthals lived at the same time, and many people today carry a small share of Neanderthal DNA."]],
atom:[["Chemical bonds","Atoms join by sharing electrons in covalent bonds or by transferring them in ionic bonds. Table salt is held together by ionic bonds between sodium and chlorine, while water is held together by covalent bonds."]],
grav:[["Gravitational waves","Einstein predicted that accelerating masses send ripples through spacetime. In 2015 the LIGO detectors recorded these gravitational waves for the first time, from two black holes merging over a billion light-years away."]],
cell:[["Organelles","Inside cells with a nucleus are smaller structures called organelles. Mitochondria release energy from food, ribosomes build proteins and, in plants, chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis."]],
light:[["Bending light","Light changes direction when it passes from one material to another, which is called refraction. Lenses in eyeglasses, cameras and telescopes use refraction to focus light, and rainbows form when sunlight is refracted and reflected in raindrops."]],
ww2:[["The war in the Pacific","Japan attacked Pearl Harbor and European and American colonies across Southeast Asia in December 1941. Major battles followed at Midway, Guadalcanal and Leyte Gulf, and the war ended after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945."]],
renais:[["Northern Renaissance","The movement spread north to the Low Countries, Germany and England. Painters such as Jan van Eyck and Albrecht Dürer, and writers such as Erasmus and Shakespeare, carried it in new directions."]],
maya:[["The Maya calendar","The Maya used several linked calendars, including a 260-day ritual cycle and a 365-day solar year. Their Long Count calendar completed a major cycle in December 2012, which the Maya themselves did not regard as the end of the world."]],
silk:[["Travelers","The Venetian merchant Marco Polo described his journey to the court of Kublai Khan in the late 1200s, and the Moroccan scholar Ibn Battuta traveled across much of Asia and Africa in the 1300s."]],
space:[["Cooperation","The rivalry eased over time. In 1975 American and Soviet spacecraft docked in orbit during the Apollo-Soyuz mission, and the two countries later became partners in the International Space Station."]],
everest:[["Measuring the peak","The Great Trigonometrical Survey of India identified it as the world’s highest peak in 1852, and it was named in 1865 after the surveyor George Everest. China and Nepal jointly announced its current height of 8,848.86 m in 2020."]],
sahara:[["The Green Sahara","Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, shifts in Earth’s orbit brought stronger monsoon rains. Lakes, grasslands and animals such as hippos covered parts of the Sahara, as rock paintings show."]],
pacific:[["Ocean currents","Huge circular currents called gyres turn in the Pacific. El Niño, a periodic warming of the eastern tropical Pacific, shifts rainfall around the world, bringing floods to some regions and drought to others."]],
nile:[["Two rivers","The Nile has two main branches. The White Nile flows from the Lake Victoria region, while the Blue Nile, which supplies most of the water and silt, rises at Lake Tana in Ethiopia. They meet at Khartoum in Sudan."]],
antarc:[["Wildlife","Few animals live on the ice, but the surrounding Southern Ocean is rich in krill, which feed whales, seals and penguins. Emperor penguins breed on the sea ice through the dark winter."]],
comp:[["How a computer works","A computer stores everything as binary digits, ones and zeros. Its processor follows instructions held in memory, a design described by John von Neumann in 1945 that most computers still use."]],
print:[["Printing in Asia","Printing began much earlier in East Asia. The Chinese Diamond Sutra of 868 is the oldest dated printed book, Bi Sheng invented movable type in about 1040 and Korea printed the Jikji with metal type in 1377."]],
flight:[["Balloons and gliders","People first flew in hot-air balloons built by the Montgolfier brothers in 1783. In the 1890s Otto Lilienthal made around 2,000 glider flights, and his work inspired the Wright brothers."]],
renew:[["Falling costs","The price of solar panels fell by about 90% in the 2010s, and solar and wind became the cheapest new sources of electricity in many countries. Wind and solar together supplied over a tenth of the world’s electricity by 2023."]],
gps:[["History","The United States military launched the first GPS satellite in 1978. After a Korean airliner strayed into Soviet airspace and was shot down in 1983, the government promised to open GPS to civilian use, and full civilian accuracy came in 2000."]],
kidney:[["Kidney stones and health","Kidney stones form when minerals in urine crystallize. Drinking enough water, limiting salt and controlling blood pressure and blood sugar help protect the kidneys, since diabetes and high blood pressure are leading causes of kidney failure."]],
hormone:[["Growth and puberty","Growth hormone from the pituitary gland drives growth in childhood. At puberty, sex hormones such as estrogen and testosterone bring the changes of adolescence."]],
hygiene:[["Sanitation","Clean water and sewers have saved more lives than almost any medicine. After John Snow traced an 1854 cholera outbreak in London to a contaminated water pump, cities began building modern sewer systems."]],
malaria:[["Treatment","Quinine, from the bark of the cinchona tree, was the first effective malaria drug. The Chinese scientist Tu Youyou discovered artemisinin, now the core of modern treatment, and won a Nobel Prize in 2015."]],
allergy:[["Why allergies are rising","Allergies have become more common in many countries. One idea, the hygiene hypothesis, suggests that less early contact with microbes may leave the immune system more prone to overreact, though scientists are still testing it."]],
spine:[["Common problems","A slipped or herniated disc happens when a disc bulges and presses on a nerve, sometimes causing pain down the leg called sciatica. Scoliosis is a sideways curve of the spine that often appears in adolescence."]],
michel:[["Rivalry and legacy","Michelangelo was admired in his own lifetime as divine. He competed with Leonardo da Vinci and Raphael, and his powerful, muscular figures influenced painters and sculptors for centuries."]],
vangogh:[["Fame after death","Van Gogh died at 37 after a gunshot wound. His sister-in-law Johanna van Gogh-Bonger promoted his work, and the Van Gogh Museum in Amsterdam now holds the largest collection of his paintings."]],
picasso:[["Many periods","Picasso’s style kept changing. His Blue Period of about 1901 to 1904 showed poverty and sadness in shades of blue, and the Rose Period that followed used warmer colors and circus performers."]],
fairy:[["What the tales mean","Fairy tales often follow a pattern in which a young hero leaves home, faces trials and is rewarded. Scholars have studied them as lessons about growing up, and many tale types are thousands of years old."]],
anim:[["How it is made","Traditional animation draws each frame by hand, usually 12 or 24 drawings per second of film. Stop-motion photographs models moved a little at a time, and computer animation builds characters as 3D models."]],
ballet:[["Ballet worldwide","Ballet is now danced on every continent, from the Bolshoi in Moscow to the Royal Ballet in London and the National Ballet of Cuba."]]
});

;
